why:
the publisher's globally-unique id, used to attribute a received message to its sender.

SHIPPED 2026-09-25 (the phase-467 RMW gap-closure design study's Q1, step 2 and step 3). The row was `gap`/`adopt` and named three things owed; all three landed, so the verdict moves to `divergence` on what is left, which is the SHAPE.

THE DIVERGENCE, and its constraint: upstream is `const rmw_gid_t & rclcpp::PublisherBase::get_gid()` — a reference, no arguments, cannot fail. Ours is `Result get_gid(uint8_t (&out_gid)[GID_SIZE]) const` (`packages/api/nros-cpp/include/rclcpp/publisher.hpp`). RFC-0018: no exceptions and no heap, so a value that may be UNAVAILABLE leaves through caller-placed storage and the verdict through the return code. It must be able to be unavailable — XRCE-DDS and uORB NULL the vtable slot — and a reference has nowhere to put that answer. The array REFERENCE rather than a pointer is deliberate: it makes the width a compile-time fact at every call site, which a `uint8_t *` does not.

HOW A USER FINDS OUT: a COMPILE ERROR. `const rmw_gid_t& g = pub->get_gid();` does not convert to a `Result`, and the no-argument call does not type-check.

WHAT IS THERE, end to end (measured, not reasoned — the tests are `packages/rmw/cffi/tests/publisher_gid.rs` and the `get_gid` assertions in `packages/rmw/cffi/tests/rust_adapter.rs`):
  * `nros_rmw::Publisher::get_gid`, defaulting to `Err(TransportError::Unsupported)` so every existing impl rides the default;
  * `nros_rmw_cffi::CffiPublisher::get_gid`, which calls the `get_gid_for_publisher` slot and answers `Unsupported` where it is NULL — the NULL-slot discipline `rmw_vtable.h` states for `get_serialization_format`;
  * the Rust-adapter trampoline, which was MISSING and is the reason a Rust backend with a real identity could not report one: the adapter table left `get_gid_for_publisher` NULL, so zenoh's answer reached no language surface;
  * `ZenohPublisher::get_gid`, which returns the 16-byte attachment gid zero-extended through `nros_core::pad_publisher_gid`;
  * the three language accessors — `rclc_publisher_get_gid` (C), `nros::Publisher<M>::get_gid` (C++), `EmbeddedPublisher::get_gid` / `EmbeddedRawPublisher::get_gid` (Rust).

NEVER AN ALL-ZERO GID. That is what the staged `rmw_gid_t` holds before the call, so returning it on the NULL-slot path would make "this backend has no identity for this publisher" read exactly like an identity — the RFC-0089 Part I shape this accessor exists on the loud side of. Asserted directly, and against the failing-slot case too: a slot that HAS the function and fails is a different fact from a slot that is absent, and only the second is a property of the image.

THREE BOUNDS, stated on every accessor's own doc comment and not only here:
  1. the width is 24, upstream's `RMW_GID_STORAGE_SIZE` (the same study's Q1(a));
  2. how many of those bytes MEAN anything is a BACKEND property — zenoh fills 16 and Cyclone fills a 16-byte DDS writer GUID, both zero-extended by the one spelling of the padding, and gids from two different backends in one image are never comparable;
  3. a gid from here and a gid from a TAKE (`MessageInfo::publisher_gid`) are one TYPE and one VALUE on every backend that produces either (issue 1495, closed 2026-10-03): zenoh derives both as XXH3-128 of the entity's liveliness keyexpr, `rmw_zenoh_cpp`'s own derivation, and Cyclone reports the writer GUID on both sides through `take_with_info`. Measured against live peers in `graph_interop` (`zenoh_publisher_gid_is_the_one_a_stock_peer_reads_and_reports`, `cyclone_take_gid_is_the_publishers_graph_gid`): our `get_gid`, a stock subscriber's take (zenoh) and `ros2 topic info --verbose` agree.

The C accessor takes a `gid_capacity` and checks it; see `c:publisher_get_gid` for why. The `implementation_identifier` half of upstream's `rmw_gid_t` is deliberately NOT carried per publisher — see the same row.
